    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Compaction bands (CBs) are thin, tabular structures of decreased porosity and permeability in sandstone, which can form pervasive arrays that exert substantial fluid-flow effects at scales relevant to production, storage and contaminant migration. To accurately model these impacts requires knowing CB porosity and permeability, which can be difficult and expensive to measure directly in the lab owing to the limited thickness (~1 cm) of the bands. Reliable and inexpensive estimates can be obtained, however, by conducting Lattice-Boltzmann flow simulations on 3-D pore structure realizations generated from the results of simple thin-section image analysis techniques. We demonstrate the efficacy of this computational rock physics approach for CBs abundantly exposed in the Aztec Sandstone of southeastern Nevada—an exhumed analog for eolian aquifers and reservoirs. Given that the Aztec is weakly cemented and contains CBs generally both thin and fragile, the 2-D image-based flow-property estimation methods described here are particularly useful. Calibrated estimates of porosity and permeability from the method correspond well with available measurements from the Aztec for both CBs (<10% and <10 mD) and matrix (~25% and ~1,500 mD).",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
